her new feature that we  introduced was with pH information so  it's sometimes nice to know this  information at the food type level so we  have four food types we have PHS and you  can sort through these things and search  through them using implicit entity  syntax so here for example like you can  find basic foods just by using greater  than or you can find acidic foods using  less equal than for example and you can  also use between to find maybe save sort  of neutral foods and in a particular  range or you can also use take largest  and take smallest to find the extreme  values and those sorts of things these  values have been aggregated over more  detailed information that comes from our  data source and so you can see some  sometimes there's some variation between  different kinds like here from different  varieties of apples they vary a little  bit it's also nice to see and and group  things on more of a food type group  levels so you can sort of zoom out and  see things in bigger bigger clumps of  foods that way so that's kind of a nice  use of number line plot to see that  going a little further one of the other  big things we introduced last year was  packaged food ingredients and we had  tens of thousands of foods I don't  remember exactly how many we had but we  have quite a few more now and now we  have about a hundred thousand packaged  foods in our database now and we have  all kinds of information that you would  find on a package including marketing  descriptions as well as these warning  labels and ingredient lists that you can  see here and a lot of these have very  useful information like for example  allergens and things like that and if  you you know have a dietary restriction  or an allergy or something these lists  are very handy to go through and find  out what foods you can and can't eat but  the problem with these is that they come  back to us as raw strings and there's a  lot of problems with that because  they're not really computable or not  very easily computable a lot of times  they come in as capital letters and so  they're not really all that friendly to  look at because it looks like someone's  screaming at you basically but and so  it's not really nice looking also  there's a hierarchical structure to  these things and so they're a little  difficult to work with if you want to  actually find out what ingredients are  made of other ingredients and those  sorts of things  so if you want to find maybe the high  level ingredients you'd have to do a lot  of  processing to rip out parentheses and  things like that to go dig through those  but most importantly lookups are really  difficult because you'd have to do some  kind of string matching and that sort of  thing and that becomes very difficult  very quickly so the solution to that is  that we've done it for you already and  introduced our own ingredient entities  so appears just an example of some of  the food ingredient entities you can see  here we have a list of a little less  than 5,000 and we're growing in size as  we go along and so for example with the  simple property call you can just get  back a list of these computable  ingredient entities rather than this big  string here and one thing to note about  these entities is that they come back  and we've tried to keep the order as  close as possible to what's in the given  in the actual string there - some  duplicates and things like that because  the order is pretty important because  the the first listed ingredients are  much more have there's much more of  those ingredients than there are in  subsequent ingredients so the order is  pretty important with those and there  are other properties that we have as  well which you'll see shortly but the  important thing is that um you can use  the implicit entity syntax to be able to  search through these foods pretty easily  so for example if you want to find foods  that have a certain ingredient on them  you can search through them that way and  of course you can use the contains  family of functions to be able to find  those that match a certain criteria  maybe like this one where you might be  looking for ingredients that list both  rice and beer or maybe use contains none  to say find beans that don't have salt  listed as an ingredient of course as I  mentioned before if you're trying to do  some of that string processing work to  try and figure out which ingredients are  made of other ingredients we've taken  some of that into consideration already  using this property that which we call  main ingredients which basically removes  the parentheses and takes out anything  after a statement like contains less  than 2% of and so you can do queries on  those for example here and so that sort  of gives a simplified list of  ingredients to work with  additionally we also give access to the  first listed ingredient which I  mentioned before is the one that's the  most prevalent or predominant and lastly  we also have a property for looking at  ingredient counts  that way you can search for foods that  have less than a certain number or  something like that - and like a like I  mentioned before on the other slide with  the all the contains family of functions  works on these as well so you can dig  through and search through foods that  way so that solves the lookup problem  but it's a little a little more  difficult if you want to use use it for  if you have say an allergy or something  like that and so we've introduced  entities for that as well to make it  much easier to sort through we've  introduced these dietary restriction  entities and you can see a list of them  here and we have curated lists of food  types and ingredients that you can avoid  for all of these things just given in  these properties here as you can see the  neat thing about this is that we give  multiple lists of levels of confidence  basically if you want to think of it  that way for example we have compatible  ones which are certainly allowed  incompatible ones which are definitely  not allowed and then sort of  questionable lists which are sort of  gray areas where you may have to dig  deeper and look into with all these  things and we have all this information  as well down at the food level as well  so for example you can use this to look  for lutein free foods that we have or  foods that are certainly not gluten free  as well again sometimes we get a lot of  questions about you know the differences  between the dietary restrictions and  things like that so we've also  introduced a description property that  gives sort of a nice textual description  of what these things are and why you may  do them and that sort of thing  another issue that people get confused  on how these all relate so we've also  introduced an implied dietary  restrictions property that allows you to  say that you know if a food is gluten  free then it's also wheat free if a food  is vegan it's also vegetarian and so on  and so forth another new feature that  we've introduced is with ingredient  substitutions we have a growing list of  about 2,000 ingredient substitutions and  it's it's growing and we're looking to  improve it so for example here I've just  put allspice as an example where you can  see with the implicit entity syntax we  have here you can replace it with ground  cinnamon ground cloves and ground nutmeg  but this might be a little bit difficult  to read so I think I've got  better solution for you here where I've  replaced them with images instead sort  of to integrate with the images that we  have baked into the system so you can  kind of see what these things are and  I've also included it with mouse overs  on them so you can see exactly what they  are before so right now um these are  written in terms of explicit quantities  and I believe Eric has a talk later in  the conference that it covers physical  quantities and things like that with  Michael and so we support all of these  and so you can convert